G12's history-preservation check admits a hash-mismatched historical blob in a declared source.

At warrant/differential.py:148-152, rewritten() reads the source blobs and then adds set(held), only their names,
to the preserved-history set. warrant/sources.py:78-105 reads the Git blob bytes without verifying SHA-256 against
the filename. Store.snapshot() validates local blob hashes, but these source bytes never pass that check.
Consequently an old blob can be removed locally, replaced by different bytes under its old hash filename in a
declared source, and --release still reports SAME, exit 0, history records=0/blobs=0. This violates the G12
requirement to retain n's historical bytes and docs/V3.md section 8's hash-integrity refusal. It is introduced into
the release-history check by #582, merged at 146aced7643dd3ad02dd400c5f126ee593df627d.

The fixture below uses the actual published 3.0.0a8 engine through differential.main(), no mocked publication
answers, a signed and accepted fixture release obligation citing the historical blob, and a source with an empty
records.jsonl. It compares a valid source blob, hash-mismatched source blob, and absent source. Valid passes;
corrupt also passes (the counterexample); absent refuses. No production store is mutated by the probe.
Expected: corrupt historical source data is refused, never counted as preserved history. Validate the bytes
against their addresses before crediting their presence, with regression coverage for valid, corrupt and absent.

